  "image_name": "2375621551a59f552323f2ad6ccb49e6.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how particle tracks in a detector can be reconstructed with a graph neural network. On the left, a simplified detector is shown as concentric circular tracker layers surrounded by an outer calorimeter ring. Several particles emerge from the collision point near the center and travel outward along curved paths, leaving discrete hit points where they cross the tracker layers. This panel conveys the raw tracking problem: infer each particle trajectory from scattered detector hits.\n\nOn the right, the same task is reframed as a graph problem. Hits become graph nodes, and an initial graph is built by drawing many candidate edges between nearby hits. These edges include both correct and incorrect connections, indicated by true and false labels. A GNN then performs inference to prune the graph, removing many false edges while retaining the ones that align with real trajectories, though a note indicates some misclassifications remain. In the final step, postprocessing identifies connected components in the pruned graph, and each connected component corresponds to a reconstructed track. Overall, the figure summarizes the full tracking pipeline from detector hits to separated particle tracks.",
